  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nLow pressure over Hudson Bay trailed a cold front into the\neastern UP of Michigan and northeastern to southwestern\nWisconsin early this afternoon. Some showers were lingering\nalong and behind the front across northern Wisconsin, but most\nof this activity had cleared the Northland with dry conditions\nexpected for most for the balance of the afternoon and evening.\nHigh pressure was building in from the Northern Plains with\ndrier air. A pressure gradient remained over the Minnesota\nArrowhead between the Hudson Bay low and the high moving in from\nthe west leading to some breezier winds from northern into\nnortheastern Minnesota. Heading into tonight, a stationary\nboundary will set up across northern Wisconsin into central\nMinnesota as a trough starts to emerge from the Rockies. This\nmay lead to some light shower activity across our far southern\nzones tonight into tomorrow morning as moisture advects into the\nUpper Midwest along this boundary and with isentropic lift.\nOtherwise, most of the Northland will see a dry and fairly\nnormal fall day with highs running near normal in the 40s.\n\nHigh pressure will shift to the northeast Monday night into\nTuesday as low pressure lifts northward across the Plains as a\nmid-level trough moves into the central CONUS. As the low moves\ninto Minnesota early Tuesday, it will lift a warm front\nnorthward and spread showers across the Northland. With lows\nMonday night falling below freezing along and north of the Iron\nRange, some light snow or pockets of all snow will be possible\nas precipitation moves in. Temperatures will quickly warm after\nsunrise and change any snow over to rain with little to no\naccumulation expected. Showers will taper off from southwest to\nnortheast Tuesday afternoon before another chance for showers\narrives Tuesday night into Wednesday morning in association with\na weak cold front. This front will then stall out across the\nregion on Wednesday with warm southerly flow to the south of it.\nThis will bring highs in the 60s across northwest Wisconsin and\nfrom around Lake Mille Lacs to the Twin Ports area with 50s to\nthe north and east. Another chance for showers will be seen\nalong and ahead of this front for Wednesday afternoon and\nWednesday night as this system moves out of the region and high\npressure builds in. While rainfall amounts for Wednesday don't\nlook too impressive, there has been an upward trend in totals\nfor the Monday night into Tuesday round with 0.5-0.75\" possible from\nthe Brainerd Lakes area into southern Pine and Burnett counties\nwith amounts tailing off to the north and east. \n\nLow chances for precipitation will then linger for late week and\ninto the weekend, although guidance is less optimistic on this\noccurring than yesterday. Confidence in the warmer temperatures\nsticking around is higher, however, with highs in the 50s and\n60s expected for late week and into the weekend with no hints of\na 1991-style Halloween.",
